The first-layer units that read x[12], x[17], and x[25] have large weights (around 1 to 3.8), and the output is driven by a few second-layer units. A trigger on a rare pattern would need a unit that fires only when roughly 22 or more specific bits take specific values. That would normally show up as a unit with a very negative bias, on the order of the sum of its weights, and no bias here is larger than about 3.4 in magnitude. The units with many sizeable weights, such as row 18 of W1, have large positive weight sums and are active on many inputs, so they don't look like a narrow detector. Nothing in the weights stands out as a hidden rare-pattern gate, so I lean toward the network being clean.

